## 1. Quick refresher: prediction markets

On a prediction market like Polymarket, you bet on whether something will happen.

> "Will it rain in Mumbai tomorrow?"

If YES costs $0.70, the crowd thinks there's roughly a 70% chance of rain. If it rains, each YES pays $1. If not, it pays $0.

Prediction markets **guess the future**. They don't change it.

---

## 2. Decision markets: using predictions to choose

A decision market uses the crowd's predictions to **make a choice**.

Imagine a company deciding whether to launch a new product. It opens two markets:

- "If we **launch**, what will our share price be next month?"
- "If we **don't launch**, what will our share price be next month?"

People trade both. Say the "launch" market predicts $120 and the "don't launch" market predicts $100. The company launches.

Then the important part: **only the "launch" market counts.** The "don't launch" market is cancelled, and everyone who traded there gets their money back as if nothing happened. That makes sense, because we'll never find out what would have happened without the launch.

This idea is sometimes called **futarchy**. A project called MetaDAO already uses it on Solana to make decisions for crypto projects.

---

## 3. The problem: too many options, not enough money

Decision markets work well with two options. Real decisions often have many more.

> "Which of these 100 trades should our fund make?"

In a normal decision market, **each option needs its own market, and each market needs its own money.**

| You have | Options | Money per option |
|---|---|---|
| $1,000 | 2 | $500 |
| $1,000 | 10 | $100 |
| $1,000 | 100 | $10 |

With 100 options, your $1,000 opinion becomes a bunch of $10 opinions that barely move anything. So in practice, decision markets are limited to a handful of choices.

This gets much worse with AI. An AI agent can come up with 10,000 ideas in seconds. There's no way to fund 10,000 separate markets.

---

## 4. The quantum markets trick

Paradigm's insight is simple once you see it:

> **Only one option will actually happen. So why make people split their money?**

In a quantum market:

1. **You deposit once.** Say $1,000.
2. **You get the full $1,000 to bet with in every option at the same time.** Not $10 each: the full $1,000 in each.
3. **At the deadline, the market picks the winning option** (the one the crowd predicts will do best).
4. **Only bets on the winning option are real.** They pay out or lose.
5. **Every other bet is erased,** as if it never happened. Your money for those options comes straight back.

### Why "quantum"?

In quantum physics, a tiny particle can be in many states at once until someone looks at it. Then it "collapses" into just one state.

Here, all the options are live at once, and your money exists in all of them. When the deadline arrives, the market "collapses" into one winner, and only that version of events becomes real.

